# Settings of the cyfr-locus image, read by each of its three compose
# services: `locus-runtime`, `locus-builds` (the `locus-builds` profile)
# and `locus-backends`. Copy to .env.locus to override defaults: one file
# for every role. Every setting of a Locus node is a LOCUS_RUNTIME_*,
# LOCUS_BUILDS_*, LOCUS_BACKENDS_* or LOCUS_LOG_* name. Do not put
# application credentials, the keyring, a database URL, the runtime's root
# or the control plane's CYFR_LOCUS_BUILDS_KEY or CYFR_LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY
# here: a Locus node of any role refuses to start with any of them in its
# environment.
#
# A node serves the runtime when it holds the runtime's key, the builds
# service when it holds a builds key and the backends service when it
# holds a backends key, and refuses to start with none. A node that holds
# the runtime's key and either other key refuses to start too: the
# runtime's runners have no network, so it shares no container, key or
# network with a role that has open egress. The keys are not set here.
# Compose passes the project .env's LOCUS_RUNTIME_KEY, the key `cyfr init`
# derives from the runtime's root for LOCUS_RUNTIME_SERVICE_ID, to the
# runtime, the project .env's CYFR_LOCUS_BUILDS_KEY, the key cyfr signs
# every build request with, to the builds service as its LOCUS_BUILDS_KEY,
# and the project .env's CYFR_LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY to the backends service as
# its LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY: each 32 bytes as 64 hexadecimal digits, which
# `cyfr init` mints. A node run without compose is given its key itself
# (integration-guide.md, "Running a worker outside Compose").

# ————— the release's log setting —————
#
# The level (a Logger level; default info) and format (`text`, or `json`
# for structured logs: Datadog, Splunk, ELK, Loki) of every role's node.
# A runner of the runtime reads neither: it logs at the default level, as
# plain text to standard error.
# LOCUS_LOG_LEVEL=info
# LOCUS_LOG_FORMAT=json

# ————— the runtime service —————
#
# What identifies the runtime is not set here: compose gives the service
# its id, its key and the URL of cyfr's host API from the project .env
# (documented in .env.example), and fixes the address and port its
# listener binds, over anything this file sets.

# The runner pool. Every subtree runs in a runner: a VM of its own under
# a pooled uid, started by cyfr-locus, holding no key. The service keeps
# this many fresh runners spawned ahead (each one a VM booting from
# nothing, so a bigger pool absorbs bursts at the cost of memory; the
# image has eight runner uids).
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_POOL_SIZE=4

# How long a runner that completed cleanly is kept idle for its athanor's
# next subtree, in milliseconds, before it is retired.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_IDLE_TTL_MS=30000

# How far past its assignment's deadline a runner may live before it halts
# its VM, in milliseconds: the bound on a guest that ignores its deadline.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_WATCHDOG_GRACE_MS=5000

# How long a runner being ended (a kill, an unclean completion) is given to
# report what it holds before its process group is killed, in milliseconds.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_RELEASE_GRACE_MS=2000

# The memory bound cyfr-locus holds each runner to, in bytes: its VM,
# every guest's linear memory, the pages of its home and the kernel memory
# charged to it, together. A runner that reaches it is ended whole and
# never reused, and the service reports its open runs. A whole number of
# bytes from 16777216 (16 MiB) to 1099511627776 (1 TiB), the keeper's own
# range; there is no value that means no bound. The default is roughly
# twice the largest runner peak under the seed components, as
# tests/locus-runtime-image/memory.py --measure records it: room for two
# or three guests at the default 64 MiB of linear memory, not one at the
# 256 MiB ceiling, so a deployment that consents to more raises it, and
# the container's limit with it (LOCUS_RUNTIME_MEMORY_LIMIT in the project
# .env: 8 runner uids x this bound + 1 GiB for the service).
#
# The bound needs the service's `security_opt: writable-cgroups=true`
# (docker-compose.yml; Docker Engine 28 or later on a cgroup v2 host).
# Without it the service starts no runner and logs, naming the option, that
# it cannot bound one: no component runs, and none runs unbounded.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_MEMORY_BYTES=402653184

# The runners start only through the cyfr-locus channel the service's
# entrypoint gives it: a service started without it refuses to boot,
# naming cyfr-locus.

# Where the keeper's relays attach: a directory only the service's user
# can enter. The image's tmpfs.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_ATTACH_DIR=/run/locus

# ————— the builds service —————

# The address and port the builds service listens on inside the container.
# In compose the container is attached to the locus-builds network alone,
# so every interface is that network; a builder outside compose sets the
# one address to bind. CYFR_LOCUS_BUILDS_URL in .env names the same port.
# LOCUS_BUILDS_BIND=0.0.0.0
# LOCUS_BUILDS_PORT=4100

# Concurrent toolchain builds (cargo/npm: a CPU core and hundreds of MB
# each, for minutes), in all and for one athanor (1..1024). A build past
# either cap is refused as at capacity, never queued. Each build runs under
# a uid of its own from the image's pool of 16.
# LOCUS_BUILDS_MAX_CONCURRENT=2
# LOCUS_BUILDS_MAX_CONCURRENT_PER_TENANT=1

# A build's deadline in milliseconds (1000..600000). Past it every process
# the build started is killed and the build is refused as timed out.
# LOCUS_BUILDS_TIMEOUT_MS=270000

# A build's memory bound in bytes (16777216..1099511627776, 16 MiB to
# 1 TiB; the default is 1 GiB). Every build runs in a cgroup of its own
# held to this bound: its processes, what it writes to its home and the
# kernel memory charged to it, together, with no swap. A build that reaches
# it loses every process at once and is refused with the `memory` class,
# naming the bound; other builds and the service are untouched. There is no
# value that means "no bound". The shipped seeds build within 356-564 MiB
# (748 MiB with a cold cache).
#
# The bound needs the service's `security_opt: writable-cgroups=true`
# (docker-compose.yml; Docker Engine 28 or later on a cgroup v2 host).
# Without it every build is refused as `unavailable`, naming the option,
# and none runs unbounded.
# LOCUS_BUILDS_MEMORY_BYTES=1073741824

# The container's own limits are compose's, which reads them from the
# project .env, not from this file: LOCUS_BUILDS_MEMORY_LIMIT (default 4G)
# and LOCUS_BUILDS_CPU_LIMIT (default 2). Nothing but the container's limit
# bounds the service itself, so the limit holds every concurrent build at
# its bound and the service beside them:
#
#   LOCUS_BUILDS_MEMORY_LIMIT >=
#     LOCUS_BUILDS_MAX_CONCURRENT x (LOCUS_BUILDS_MEMORY_BYTES + 1 GiB)
#   default: 2 x (1 GiB + 1 GiB) = 4 GiB
#
# The 1 GiB is measured (tests/builder-image/headroom.py, at cpus: 2). The
# service idles at 145-185 MiB and takes the most answering a result at
# the wire's bound of 64 MiB of outputs, when it holds the output archive,
# its files, their base64 and the answer's line at once: 620-870 MiB for
# one such result, 910-1250 MiB for two answered together while both builds
# hold 930 MiB of their bounds, and about 1.6 GiB if two coincided exactly.
# Raise the limit with either setting; a limit below the sum lets the
# kernel kill a build, or the service, for the container's limit instead of
# ending the one build that passed its own.

# The Cargo home whose registry cache a Rust build starts from. The image
# sets it to the seed it ships (/opt/cyfr/cargo-seed).
# LOCUS_BUILDS_CARGO_SEED=/opt/cyfr/cargo-seed

# ————— the backends service —————
#
# A node given LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY serves the stdio MCP servers CYFR
# registers: 64 hexadecimal digits, the same key as the control plane's
# copy, CYFR_LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY in the project .env. Every backend runs
# under a uid of its own from the keeper's `backends` pool.

# The address and port the backends service listens on inside the
# container. CYFR_LOCUS_BACKENDS_URL, which compose sets for cyfr, names
# the same port.
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_BIND=0.0.0.0
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_PORT=4101

# Calls that may await one backend at a time (1..1024). A call past it is
# answered as the backend's error, never queued.
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_MAX_IN_FLIGHT=32

# The bound on each step of a backend's handshake (initialize, then
# tools/list) and on one call to it, in milliseconds (1000..600000). A
# handshake past its bound ends the process, which restarts after its
# backoff; a call past its bound is answered as the backend's error.
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_INIT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_RPC_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# A backend's memory bound in bytes (16777216..1099511627776, 16 MiB to
# 1 TiB; the default is 512 MiB), charged through the keeper's cgroup as a
# build's is: its processes, what they write to their home and the kernel
# memory charged to them, together, with no swap. A backend that reaches
# it loses every process at once and restarts after its backoff. There is
# no value that means "no bound".
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_MEMORY_BYTES=536870912
